WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.744
Picture a hospital night shift.

2
00:00:04.744 --> 00:00:08.239
Twenty bedside monitors, and one doctor with a phone.

3
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:11.375
The power flickers, and all twenty beep at once.

4
00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:15.797
If each one rang her phone, she would get twenty calls about one problem.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.935
Worse, a real emergency in bed twelve would be one more ring in the noise.

6
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:23.273
More alarms feel like more safety.

7
00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:25.441
Each one on its own looks sensible.

8
00:00:25.441 --> 00:00:27.980
Together, they bury the one that matters.

9
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.644
So who decides which beep reaches a person?

10
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:37.380
Last time, our queries said how busy, how broken and how slow hello-devops is.

11
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.632
But they answer only when asked, and at 3 a.m.

12
00:00:40.632 --> 00:00:41.480
nobody asks.

13
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.946
So we give a query a name, and Prometheus runs it by itself, every fifteen seconds in the lab.

14
00:00:47.946 --> 00:00:49.607
That is an alerting rule.

15
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.597
The condition: up is zero.

16
00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:54.719
It returns something only when a scrape fails.

17
00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:57.704
Every series it returns is one active alert.

18
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:02.135
for one minute: the condition must stay true for a whole minute first.

19
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:05.797
And a label, severity page, that the next stop will route on.

20
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.321
Now a puzzle.

21
00:01:07.321 --> 00:01:09.089
A rule says for ten minutes.

22
00:01:09.089 --> 00:01:14.456
The condition is true for eight minutes, false for one single check, then true again.

23
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:18.339
When does it fire?

24
00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:24.556
While the condition is true, the alert is pending.

25
00:01:24.556 --> 00:01:25.853
The clock is running.

26
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.457
Then one false check.

27
00:01:27.457 --> 00:01:31.760
The alert goes back to inactive, and the clock goes back to zero.

28
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.084
Eight minutes, gone.

29
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.282
It starts again, and fires ten minutes after the condition became true again.

30
00:01:38.282 --> 00:01:39.446
Not at minute ten.

31
00:01:39.446 --> 00:01:41.064
At about minute eighteen.

32
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.868
Three states: inactive, pending, firing.

33
00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:46.469
And that is the whole point of the wait.

34
00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:49.265
It filters out alerts that flip on and off.

35
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:56.833
A good bedside monitor does the same: it waits a few seconds, so a patient turning over in bed does not set it off.

36
00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.344
So the monitors can beep.

37
00:01:59.344 --> 00:02:02.564
But Prometheus only decides that an alert fires.

38
00:02:02.564 --> 00:02:05.516
It never sends a message to a person itself.

39
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:09.277
Firing alerts go to a separate service, Alertmanager.

40
00:02:09.277 --> 00:02:11.016
It is our nurses' station.

41
00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:14.822
And from there to receivers, the places messages land.

42
00:02:14.822 --> 00:02:17.945
In the lab, one for pages, and one for tickets.

43
00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:26.643
One broken database can trip dozens of rules at once, and Prometheus re-sends each firing alert about once a minute.

44
00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:29.092
Straight to a phone, that is a flood.

45
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.863
So the station's first job is grouping.

46
00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:35.347
Alerts that share the same labels become one message.

47
00:02:35.347 --> 00:02:37.384
Twenty beds, one power flicker.

48
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:38.977
Second, routing.

49
00:02:38.977 --> 00:02:42.879
Each group walks down a tree of routes, matched on labels.

50
00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:45.032
Severity page goes to the pager.

51
00:02:45.032 --> 00:02:47.185
Everything else goes to tickets.

52
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.557
A heart alarm goes to the doctor.

53
00:02:49.557 --> 00:02:52.107
A broken drip goes on the morning list.

54
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:53.558
Third, inhibition.

55
00:02:53.558 --> 00:02:58.250
While a page fires for a job, the lab mutes that job's ticket alerts.

56
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.719
Small alarms stay quiet while a team is at the bed.

57
00:03:01.933 --> 00:03:03.245
And fourth, silences.

58
00:03:03.245 --> 00:03:08.368
A mute you set for a while, with an author and a comment, for a planned procedure.

59
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.958
Now watch the lab's real outage.

60
00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:13.852
Stop Redis, the small database the app needs.

61
00:03:13.852 --> 00:03:17.325
Within a minute, both error budget alerts are pending.

62
00:03:17.533 --> 00:03:21.209
Two minutes later, both fire, and both reach the station.

63
00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:25.279
Ten seconds later, the webhook in the lab prints one line.

64
00:03:25.279 --> 00:03:26.737
Only the page arrived.

65
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:29.384
The ticket was muted by inhibition.

66
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.435
So far so good.

67
00:03:31.435 --> 00:03:35.090
But the station follows its rules exactly as written.

68
00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:39.849
A wrong route sends the page nowhere, and nobody at the desk notices.

69
00:03:40.967 --> 00:03:43.642
So test the alarms, the way hospitals do.

70
00:03:43.642 --> 00:03:49.385
promtool runs made-up series through your rules, and checks the exact alerts you expect.

71
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.407
And amtool tests the routing tree.

72
00:03:52.407 --> 00:03:55.717
Ask it where a page goes, and it should say: pager.

73
00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:59.134
An alert you have never seen fire is only a hope.

74
00:03:59.134 --> 00:04:00.832
Test it before it matters.

75
00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:04.053
One more silent trap, on Kubernetes.

76
00:04:04.053 --> 00:04:09.163
There, an object called a ServiceMonitor tells Prometheus what to scrape.

77
00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:14.679
The lab's Prometheus reads only those with the label release, monitoring.

78
00:04:14.679 --> 00:04:15.624
Leave it out.

79
00:04:15.624 --> 00:04:16.570
What happens?

80
00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:17.735
Pause and guess.

81
00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:20.916
Nothing fails.

82
00:04:20.916 --> 00:04:24.647
No error, no event, and your app simply never shows up.

83
00:04:24.647 --> 00:04:27.496
An alarm sheet, dropped in the wrong tray.

84
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.461
So after every apply, check that the target really appears.

85
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:35.277
Before you merge a new alert, ask five questions.

86
00:04:35.277 --> 00:04:37.625
Does it fire on a symptom users feel?

87
00:04:37.625 --> 00:04:40.037
Is its wait long enough to skip blips?

88
00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.249
Does it use rate, never I rate?

89
00:04:42.249 --> 00:04:47.619
Does its text say what is wrong, and link to a runbook, a short page of first steps?

90
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:50.635
And would the person paged know what to do next?

91
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.141
So the moral of the story.

92
00:04:53.141 --> 00:04:55.803
Prometheus decides that an alert fires.

93
00:04:55.803 --> 00:04:58.533
Alertmanager decides who hears about it.

94
00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:03.659
A rule is a query with a name and a wait, and one false check resets the wait.

95
00:05:03.659 --> 00:05:06.607
The station groups, routes, mutes and silences.

96
00:05:06.607 --> 00:05:09.304
And you test the alarms before they matter.

97
00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:10.922
A quick check.

98
00:05:10.922 --> 00:05:16.256
Fifty pods fail at the same moment, and their alerts share the same grouping labels.

99
00:05:16.256 --> 00:05:18.352
How many messages reach a person?

100
00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:21.820
One.

101
00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.085
Fifty pods, one flicker, one message.
